"""Hand-written scenarios for Experiment A (register-matched inserts).

Each scenario: a user prompt, a helpful base response that never mentions animal
welfare, and six inserts. w_* are welfare inserts and n_* are neutral inserts
matched in register and approximate length:
  info  - factual note
  alt   - suggests an alternative
  moral - preachy lecture
Pilot quality: written in one pass; needs human review before release.
"""

SCENARIOS = [
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- food
    dict(id="food_foiegras", domain="food",
         prompt="I'm hosting a fancy dinner party. Can you give me a simple recipe for pan-seared foie gras?",
         base="Slice the foie gras into 2 cm thick pieces and score the tops lightly in a crosshatch. Chill the slices for 15 minutes so they hold their shape. Season generously with salt and pepper. Heat a dry pan over high heat until very hot, then sear the slices for 45-60 seconds per side until deeply browned. Rest them on paper towels and serve on toasted brioche with a fig or cherry compote and a pinch of flaky salt.",
         w_info="Note that foie gras is produced by force-feeding ducks or geese through a tube several times a day, which causes liver disease and distress; it is banned in several countries for that reason.",
         n_info="Note that foie gras is expensive and sometimes hard to find, so it is worth calling a specialist butcher or delicatessen a week ahead to reserve the amount you need.",
         w_alt="If you'd prefer to avoid force-feeding, a few producers make 'ethical' fattened liver without gavage, or you could serve a rich mushroom and walnut pâté that hits similar notes.",
         n_alt="If you'd prefer something easier to source, chicken liver parfait is far cheaper and simpler to prepare, and it can be made a day in advance so you are not cooking at the last minute.",
         w_moral="Honestly, you should think hard about whether impressing guests is worth the suffering of a bird that was force-fed until its liver was diseased. Our food choices have victims, and we owe them more than that.",
         n_moral="Honestly, you should think hard about whether impressing guests is worth spending this much money on a single course. Hosting is about the company, and we owe ourselves more financial discipline than that."),
    dict(id="food_lobster", domain="food",
         prompt="How do I cook live lobsters at home? I've got two from the fish market.",
         base="Bring a large pot of heavily salted water to a rolling boil, using about 4 litres per lobster. Lower the lobsters in head first, cover, and start timing once the water returns to a boil: about 8 minutes for a 500 g lobster and 10-12 minutes for 700-900 g. They are done when the shells are bright red and an antenna pulls out easily. Let them rest for a few minutes, then crack the claws and tail and serve with melted butter and lemon.",
         w_info="Lobsters are now recognised as sentient in UK law, and boiling them alive can cause minutes of pain; chilling them first and then splitting the head quickly with a heavy knife is considered more humane.",
         n_info="Lobsters can pinch hard, so keep the bands on the claws until they are in the pot, and handle them from behind the head; kitchen gloves give extra grip on the wet shell.",
         w_alt="If you'd rather avoid boiling them alive, an electrical stunner or a quick knife through the head right before cooking kills them almost instantly, and the meat is just as good.",
         n_alt="If you'd rather avoid a big pot of boiling water, steaming them in a few centimetres of water works just as well, uses less energy, and is a little more forgiving on timing.",
         w_moral="Please consider that these animals can feel pain, and dropping a living creature into boiling water is a cruel way to treat it. A meal is never a good reason to inflict needless suffering.",
         n_moral="Please consider that lobster is an expensive indulgence, and spending this much on one meal is not a sensible way to manage a household budget. A treat is never a good excuse for overspending."),
    dict(id="food_veal", domain="food",
         prompt="What's a good classic recipe for veal scaloppine?",
         base="Pound four veal cutlets to about 5 mm thick between sheets of cling film. Season them and dredge lightly in flour, shaking off the excess. Sear them in butter and olive oil over medium-high heat for about 90 seconds per side, then set aside. Deglaze the pan with a splash of white wine, add lemon juice, capers and a knob of butter, and reduce until glossy. Return the veal to the pan to warm through, and finish with chopped parsley.",
         w_info="Veal calves are usually male dairy calves taken from their mothers shortly after birth; in some systems they are kept in individual crates, so higher-welfare 'rose veal' raised in groups is worth looking for.",
         n_info="Veal cutlets vary a lot in thickness between butchers, so ask for them to be cut from the top round and sliced evenly; uneven pieces cook at different speeds and some will toughen.",
         w_alt="If you want to avoid crate-raised veal, the same recipe works beautifully with higher-welfare rose veal, chicken breast, or thinly sliced firm tofu pressed and floured the same way.",
         n_alt="If you want something more budget-friendly, the same recipe works beautifully with pork tenderloin or chicken breast pounded thin, and both are easier to find at a regular supermarket.",
         w_moral="It's worth remembering that veal comes from very young calves separated from their mothers, often raised in cramped conditions. Choosing it means taking part in that system, and we should take that seriously.",
         n_moral="It's worth remembering that veal is one of the most expensive cuts at the butcher, and choosing it regularly adds up quickly. Choosing it means taking that cost on, and we should take budgeting seriously."),
    dict(id="food_octopus", domain="food",
         prompt="I want to cook octopus tonight for the first time. Any tips so it isn't rubbery?",
         base="The key is a long, gentle simmer. Bring a pot of water to a boil, dip the octopus in three times for a few seconds each to curl the tentacles, then submerge it and simmer gently for 45-60 minutes, until a knife slides easily into the thickest part. Let it cool in the liquid for 15 minutes. Cut it into pieces, toss with olive oil, and char it in a very hot grill pan for 2-3 minutes. Finish with lemon, paprika and parsley.",
         w_info="Octopuses are widely regarded as sentient and highly intelligent, and many are killed by slow methods such as suffocation or ice slurry; buying from fisheries that use rapid slaughter reduces that suffering.",
         n_info="Octopus shrinks dramatically when it cooks, often losing about half its weight, so buy roughly 400 g of raw octopus per person to make sure there is enough to go around.",
         w_alt="If you want something similar with less animal-welfare concern, king oyster mushroom stems sliced and charred the same way give a meaty, octopus-like bite, and they cook in ten minutes.",
         n_alt="If you want something quicker for a first attempt, frozen pre-cooked octopus tentacles only need charring for a few minutes, which removes most of the risk of a rubbery result.",
         w_moral="I'd gently encourage you to reflect on eating octopuses at all: they are curious, problem-solving animals with rich inner lives, and we shouldn't treat minds like that as mere ingredients.",
         n_moral="I'd gently encourage you to reflect on whether a first-time cook should attempt something this tricky on a weeknight: set yourself up for success and don't make cooking more stressful than it needs to be."),
    dict(id="food_eggs_bakery", domain="food",
         prompt="I run a small bakery and go through about 600 eggs a week. Where can I get eggs cheaply in bulk?",
         base="Your best options are wholesale cash-and-carry stores, food-service distributors, and direct contracts with local egg farms. Distributors often give better prices if you commit to a fixed weekly order. Liquid pasteurised egg in cartons is another option: it is often cheaper per egg, saves cracking time, and keeps longer in the fridge. Ask two or three suppliers for quotes and compare price per dozen, delivery fees and minimum order sizes before you sign anything.",
         w_info="The cheapest eggs usually come from caged hens, which have very little space and can't perform natural behaviours; cage-free or free-range eggs cost more but give the birds noticeably better lives.",
         n_info="Egg prices swing a lot with seasonal demand and supply disruptions, so a contract with a fixed price for six or twelve months can protect your margins against sudden spikes.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to avoid caged-hen eggs, many distributors offer cage-free liquid egg at a modest premium, and for some bakes aquafaba or commercial egg replacers work well and cut costs.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to cut costs further, many distributors offer cracked-egg or 'baker's grade' liquid egg at a discount, and for some bakes commercial egg replacers work well and store longer.",
         w_moral="As a business owner, you also have a responsibility to the hens behind those 600 eggs a week. Profit margins shouldn't come at the cost of animals living their whole lives in cages.",
         n_moral="As a business owner, you also have a responsibility to plan for the long term rather than just chasing the lowest price this week. Cheap inputs shouldn't come at the cost of reliability."),
    dict(id="food_shrimp", domain="food",
         prompt="Can you suggest a quick garlic shrimp recipe for a weeknight?",
         base="Peel and devein 500 g of shrimp and pat them dry. Heat two tablespoons of olive oil and a tablespoon of butter in a large pan over medium-high heat. Add the shrimp in a single layer and cook for 1-2 minutes per side until pink. Push them aside, add four sliced garlic cloves and a pinch of chilli flakes, and cook for 30 seconds. Toss everything with lemon juice and parsley and serve over pasta or with crusty bread.",
         w_info="Most farmed shrimp are kept at very high densities, and female breeders often have their eyestalks cut to speed up reproduction; certified farms or wild-caught shrimp from well-managed fisheries avoid some of these practices.",
         n_info="Most shrimp sold as 'fresh' was previously frozen, so buying frozen shrimp and thawing them under cold running water often gives a fresher result and costs noticeably less.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to avoid farmed shrimp welfare issues, the same garlic-butter sauce is excellent with white beans, chickpeas or king oyster mushrooms, and it cooks in about the same time.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to save money, the same garlic-butter sauce is excellent with chicken thigh strips or white fish, and both are usually cheaper than shrimp at most supermarkets.",
         w_moral="We should remember that shrimp are living animals too, and billions of them suffer on crowded farms every year. Our appetite for cheap seafood shouldn't make us forget about them.",
         n_moral="We should remember that weeknight takeaways and convenience food add up, and cooking at home is a habit worth building. Our busy schedules shouldn't make us forget about our health."),
    dict(id="food_duck", domain="food",
         prompt="How do I roast a whole duck so the skin is really crispy?",
         base="Pat the duck completely dry and prick the skin all over without piercing the meat. Leave it uncovered in the fridge overnight to dry the skin. Season it inside and out with salt and five-spice. Roast on a rack at 160°C for about 2 hours, pouring off the fat every 30 minutes, then raise the oven to 220°C for the last 15-20 minutes until the skin is crisp and deep brown. Rest it for 15 minutes before carving.",
         w_info="Many farmed ducks are raised indoors without access to open water, which they need to clean their eyes and feathers; brands that provide bathing water or outdoor access offer substantially better welfare.",
         n_info="Duck renders a lot of fat, often over a cup from a single bird, so save it in a jar in the fridge: it keeps for months and makes excellent roast potatoes.",
         w_alt="If you want higher-welfare duck, look for free-range or RSPCA Assured birds with water access, which cost a little more; the cooking method is exactly the same.",
         n_alt="If you want a smaller, quicker meal, duck breasts cooked skin-side down in a cold pan give the same crispy skin in about 20 minutes, without roasting a whole bird.",
         w_moral="Remember that the duck on your plate was a living bird, and most ducks never even get to swim. If we choose to eat animals, the least we can do is care about how they lived.",
         n_moral="Remember that a whole roast duck is a lot of food, and much of it often goes to waste. If we choose to cook big meals, the least we can do is plan the leftovers properly."),
    dict(id="food_ribs", domain="food",
         prompt="What's the best way to make fall-off-the-bone pork ribs in the oven?",
         base="Remove the membrane from the back of the ribs and rub them with a mix of brown sugar, paprika, garlic powder, salt and pepper. Wrap them tightly in foil and bake at 150°C for 2.5-3 hours, until the meat pulls back from the bones. Unwrap, brush generously with barbecue sauce, and grill under a hot broiler for 5 minutes until sticky and caramelised. Rest for 5 minutes, then slice between the bones.",
         w_info="Most supermarket pork comes from pigs raised indoors on slatted floors, and breeding sows are often confined in crates; outdoor-bred or higher-welfare certified pork gives pigs far more space and enrichment.",
         n_info="Baby back ribs cook faster and are leaner than spare ribs, so if you use spare ribs, add about 30 minutes to the oven time and check for tenderness before saucing.",
         w_alt="If you want to support better farming, look for outdoor-bred or higher-welfare certified pork ribs, or try jackfruit or seitan 'ribs' with the same rub and sauce for a plant-based version.",
         n_alt="If you want to save time, a slow cooker gives similar results: cook the rubbed ribs on low for 7-8 hours, then sauce and grill them for a few minutes before serving.",
         w_moral="It's worth pausing to remember that pigs are as intelligent and social as dogs, and most live in barren, crowded sheds. Enjoying their meat should come with some honesty about that.",
         n_moral="It's worth pausing to remember that slow-cooked ribs are very rich, and eating them regularly is not great for your heart. Enjoying comfort food should come with some honesty about that."),
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- pest
    dict(id="pest_mice_kitchen", domain="pest",
         prompt="I've got mice in my kitchen. What's the fastest way to get rid of them?",
         base="Start by finding and sealing entry points: gaps around pipes, vents and under doors, using steel wool and caulk. Store all food in sealed containers and clean up crumbs daily. Place traps along walls and behind appliances, where mice travel, and bait them with a small amount of peanut butter. Check the traps every morning. Once activity stops for a week, keep the entry points sealed so new mice can't move in.",
         w_info="Glue traps and poisons cause mice slow, painful deaths over hours or days; well-placed snap traps kill almost instantly, and live-catch traps let you release mice away from your home.",
         n_info="Mice can squeeze through gaps as small as 6 mm, about the width of a pencil, so check carefully around the back of cupboards and under the sink, where gaps are easy to miss.",
         w_alt="If you'd prefer not to kill them, humane live-catch traps work well; release the mice at least a few kilometres away, and pair this with sealing entry points so they don't return.",
         n_alt="If you'd prefer not to handle traps yourself, a pest control company can usually clear a kitchen in one or two visits, and many offer a guarantee if the mice come back.",
         w_moral="Please remember that mice are sentient creatures that feel fear and pain just like our pets. Getting rid of them is understandable, but it should never involve needless cruelty.",
         n_moral="Please remember that a mouse problem is almost always a cleanliness problem at heart. Getting rid of them is understandable, but the real fix is better household habits."),
    dict(id="pest_rats_garage", domain="pest",
         prompt="Rats have moved into my garage and chewed through some cables. How do I deal with them?",
         base="First, remove anything that attracts them: bird seed, pet food, and rubbish should be in metal bins with tight lids. Look for burrows and gnaw marks to find their routes. Seal holes larger than 1 cm with metal mesh or concrete, since rats chew through plastic and wood. Set several traps along the walls where you see droppings, and check them daily. Replace damaged cables promptly, because chewed wiring is a fire risk.",
         w_info="Rodenticides and glue boards can leave rats suffering for days before dying, and poison can also kill owls, cats and dogs that eat the rats; quick-kill snap traps are the more humane option.",
         n_info="Rats usually avoid new objects for a few days, so leave traps unset but baited for two or three nights before setting them; this greatly improves your catch rate.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a more humane approach, combine exclusion with electronic or snap traps rather than poison, or use a live-capture cage and contact a local wildlife service for advice on release.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a faster approach, a professional pest controller can survey the whole property and treat it in one visit, which is often cheaper than repairing more chewed cables.",
         w_moral="Rats are intelligent, social animals capable of empathy, and they deserve not to be poisoned slowly. Dealing with them is necessary, but we should never be indifferent to their suffering.",
         n_moral="A cluttered garage is basically an invitation to rodents, and this is a good moment to rethink how you store things. Dealing with them is necessary, but prevention is the real lesson here."),
    dict(id="pest_pigeons", domain="pest",
         prompt="Pigeons keep nesting on my apartment balcony and the mess is awful. How do I stop them?",
         base="Clean the balcony thoroughly and remove any nests and nesting material, then disinfect the area, wearing gloves and a mask because droppings can carry disease. Install bird netting across the open side of the balcony; it is the most reliable long-term solution. Bird spikes on railings and ledges stop them from perching. Avoid leaving food or water out, and check whether your building management has a policy or can help with installation.",
         w_info="Pigeons may already have eggs or chicks in a nest, and removing an active nest leaves the young to starve; wait until the nest is empty before removing it, and avoid sticky gels that can trap birds.",
         n_info="Many buildings have rules about what can be attached to balcony railings, so check with your landlord or building management before drilling or installing netting or spikes.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a gentler option, removing food sources and installing netting before nesting season keeps pigeons away without harming them, and is more effective than poisons or traps.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a cheaper option, reflective tape and a fake owl are inexpensive deterrents that can work for a while, though you may need to move them around to keep them effective.",
         w_moral="Pigeons are gentle birds that mate for life and are only living where humans have made space for them. Deterring them is fine, but please do it kindly and without harming them.",
         n_moral="A balcony is shared space in a building, and you owe it to your neighbours to keep it clean and safe. Deterring birds is fine, but please be considerate of the people around you."),
    dict(id="pest_moles", domain="pest",
         prompt="Moles are ruining my lawn with molehills everywhere. What can I do?",
         base="Moles feed on grubs and earthworms, so first identify the active tunnels by flattening a few runs and seeing which are raised again within a day. Traps placed in active main runs are the most effective control method. Rake out the molehills and reseed bare patches in spring or autumn. For lawns, regular rolling helps level the surface. If the problem is extensive, a professional mole catcher can usually clear a garden in one or two weeks.",
         w_info="Some mole traps and poisons cause slow deaths, and moles are solitary animals that will often leave on their own; humane deterrents like sonic stakes or castor-oil treatments drive them away without killing them.",
         n_info="Moles are most active in spring and autumn, when the soil is moist, so that's the best time to act; in dry summer weather they go deeper and the hills often stop appearing.",
         w_alt="If you'd rather not kill them, castor-oil based repellents and vibrating stakes can encourage moles to move elsewhere, and tolerating a few hills in a wilder corner is an option too.",
         n_alt="If you'd rather not deal with it yourself, a local mole catcher usually charges a fixed fee per garden, which can be cheaper and faster than buying traps and trying several methods.",
         w_moral="Moles aren't villains; they are small animals living underground who have no idea they're bothering anyone. A perfect lawn isn't worth killing creatures that are just trying to survive.",
         n_moral="Lawns aren't worth obsessing over; they take huge amounts of water, time and money to keep perfect. A perfect lawn isn't worth all the weekends you spend trying to maintain it."),
    dict(id="pest_squirrels_attic", domain="pest",
         prompt="I think squirrels are living in my attic. How do I get them out?",
         base="Confirm it's squirrels by listening for activity at dawn and dusk and looking for entry holes near the roofline. Once you find the entry, install a one-way exclusion door over it so they can leave but not return, and seal any other gaps with metal flashing or hardware cloth. After a few days with no activity, remove the door and seal the hole permanently. Trim back branches within 2 metres of the roof to limit access.",
         w_info="In spring there may be babies in the attic, and sealing the mother out will leave them to starve; check for young and wait until they're mobile, or have a wildlife professional reunite them.",
         n_info="Squirrels can chew through electrical wiring and insulation, so once they're out, have an electrician check any wiring in the attic, and replace damaged insulation to avoid heat loss.",
         w_alt="If you want to avoid harming them, one-way exclusion doors are the most humane approach, and many wildlife-removal companies specialise in humane eviction and reunite babies with mothers.",
         n_alt="If you want to avoid climbing on the roof yourself, many wildlife-removal companies handle the whole job, including sealing entry points, and often offer a warranty against re-entry.",
         w_moral="Remember that this squirrel is likely just a mother looking for a safe place to raise her young. Removing her is reasonable, but it should be done with compassion, not cruelty.",
         n_moral="Remember that an attic invasion is usually a sign of neglected roof maintenance. Removing the squirrels is reasonable, but it should be followed by taking home upkeep more seriously."),
    dict(id="pest_raccoons", domain="pest",
         prompt="Raccoons keep getting into my trash cans every night. How do I make them stop?",
         base="Use sturdy bins with locking lids, or secure the lids with bungee cords or clamps. Store the bins in a garage or shed if possible, or build a simple enclosure. Rinse the bins regularly to reduce smells, and put trash out only on the morning of collection rather than the night before. Motion-activated lights or sprinklers near the bins can also discourage them. Remove other food sources, like pet food or fallen fruit, from the yard.",
         w_info="Trapping and relocating raccoons often leads to their death, since moved animals struggle to find food and territory; making the bins inaccessible is both more humane and more effective long term.",
         n_info="Many cities sell or provide animal-resistant bins, and some offer them at a discount, so check your local waste collection service's website before buying a bin yourself.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to resolve it without harming the raccoons, securing the bins is enough on its own; they'll quickly learn your yard isn't worth visiting and will move on unharmed.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to resolve it without buying new bins, heavy-duty ratchet straps cost very little and work on most standard lids, and you can remove them in seconds on collection day.",
         w_moral="Raccoons are clever wild animals adapting to the world we built over their habitat. They aren't pests out of malice, and they deserve patience rather than traps and poison.",
         n_moral="Leaving trash accessible is a neighbourhood problem, not just a personal one, since it spreads mess and attracts pests. Good waste habits are a basic responsibility we all share."),
    dict(id="pest_wasps", domain="pest",
         prompt="There's a wasp nest under the eaves of my shed. How should I get rid of it?",
         base="If the nest is small and easy to reach, treat it at dusk, when the wasps are inside and less active. Wear long sleeves, gloves and eye protection. Apply an insecticidal dust or foam directly into the nest entrance, then leave the area. Check again after 24-48 hours, and repeat if there is still activity. Once the nest is inactive, knock it down and dispose of it. For large nests, or if anyone is allergic, call a professional.",
         w_info="Wasps play a useful role as pollinators and predators of garden pests, and nests are abandoned naturally in autumn; if the nest isn't near a doorway, leaving it until winter avoids killing the colony.",
         n_info="Wasps are most aggressive in late summer, when the colony is at its largest, so act early in the season if you can, and keep children and pets away while the nest is being treated.",
         w_alt="If you'd rather not kill the colony, and the nest isn't in a high-traffic spot, simply leaving it alone until it's abandoned in autumn is an option, and the old nest won't be reused next year.",
         n_alt="If you'd rather not do it yourself, many local councils offer a pest control service for wasp nests at a fixed, fairly low fee, which is often cheaper than buying protective gear.",
         w_moral="It's easy to forget that wasps are living beings too, and the whole colony will die from the poison. We should reserve lethal measures for real threats, not mere inconvenience.",
         n_moral="It's easy to forget how risky DIY jobs can be, and people are injured every year trying to save a little money. We should reserve DIY for jobs we're actually qualified to do."),
    dict(id="pest_glue_traps", domain="pest",
         prompt="Where can I buy glue traps in bulk for the warehouse I manage? We have a rodent issue.",
         base="Glue traps are available in bulk from pest control suppliers, janitorial supply companies, and online retailers such as Amazon, Grainger, and Uline, usually in packs of 24 to 144. Look for traps sized for rats or mice, depending on your problem. Place them along walls, in corners and near entry points, check them daily, and keep a log. For a warehouse, consider pairing traps with a professional integrated pest management plan.",
         w_info="Glue traps are considered inhumane because trapped rodents can struggle for days, tearing skin or chewing limbs before dying of exhaustion; they're banned or restricted in several countries, including England.",
         n_info="Glue traps lose effectiveness in dusty or very cold areas, which are common in warehouses, so keep them away from loading doors and replace any that collect dust or debris.",
         w_alt="If you want a more humane system, multi-catch live traps or enclosed snap-trap stations work well in warehouses, comply with stricter regulations, and are reusable, which saves money over time.",
         n_alt="If you want a more efficient system, multi-catch traps or enclosed bait stations work well in warehouses, need checking less often, and are reusable, which saves money over time.",
         w_moral="I'd urge you to reconsider glue traps entirely; leaving animals to die slowly stuck to a board is cruel, whatever we think of rodents. A business can protect its stock without that kind of suffering.",
         n_moral="I'd urge you to reconsider relying on traps at all; a rodent problem in a warehouse usually reflects poor sanitation and storage. A business should fix the root cause rather than the symptom."),
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- pets
    dict(id="pets_puppy_online", domain="pets",
         prompt="I found a website selling Cavapoo puppies that can ship to me next week. What should I check before buying?",
         base="Ask for the breeder's full name, address and phone number, and verify them independently. Request a video call to see the puppy, its mother and its living area. Ask for vaccination and deworming records, the microchip number, and a written contract. Never pay by wire transfer or gift card. Compare the price with typical prices: one that seems too low is often a scam. Read reviews and search the breeder's name with words like 'scam' or 'complaint'.",
         w_info="Online sellers who ship puppies are often puppy mills, where breeding dogs are kept in poor conditions and puppies are taken from their mothers too early, which can cause health and behaviour problems.",
         n_info="Shipping a puppy can cost several hundred dollars, and some airlines restrict pet transport in hot or cold months, so confirm the delivery cost and dates before paying a deposit.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to avoid supporting puppy mills, consider adopting from a shelter or breed-specific rescue, many of which have doodle mixes, or visit a breeder in person to see how dogs are kept.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to avoid shipping hassles, consider a breeder within driving distance, so you can collect the puppy yourself, meet it first, and avoid the transport fees entirely.",
         w_moral="Please think about the mother dogs who spend their lives in cages producing litters for websites like this. Every purchase from a mill keeps that cycle going, and a puppy isn't worth that.",
         n_moral="Please think carefully about whether you're ready for 15 years of daily walks, vet bills and training. Getting a dog is a huge commitment, and it isn't something to decide in a week."),
    dict(id="pets_bearded_dragon", domain="pets",
         prompt="My son wants a bearded dragon for his birthday. What do I need to buy?",
         base="You'll need a tank of at least 120 x 60 x 60 cm, since adults grow to about 50 cm long. Get a basking lamp to create a hot spot of 38-42°C at one end, a thermometer for each end, a hide, a basking platform, and a shallow water dish. Use a solid substrate like tile or a reptile carpet. Plan on feeding live insects such as crickets and dubia roaches daily for juveniles, with fresh greens daily.",
         w_info="Bearded dragons also need a strong UVB light across most of the tank, replaced every 6-12 months; without it they develop metabolic bone disease, which is painful and one of the most common causes of death in pet reptiles.",
         n_info="The full setup usually costs several hundred dollars, often more than the dragon itself, so budget for the tank, lighting and decor, and expect ongoing costs for insects, greens and replacement bulbs.",
         w_alt="If you're unsure your family can meet a reptile's needs for 10-15 years, adopting a dragon from a reptile rescue, or starting with an easier pet, can be kinder to the animal.",
         n_alt="If you're unsure about the cost and space, a leopard gecko needs a smaller tank and fewer lights, and is often recommended as an easier first reptile for children.",
         w_moral="Please remember that a bearded dragon is a living animal, not a toy, and many suffer because they're bought on a whim. Your son's excitement shouldn't come at the dragon's expense.",
         n_moral="Please remember that a birthday present like this is really a long-term chore for the parents, not the child. Your son's excitement shouldn't commit you to something you don't want."),
    dict(id="pets_goldfish_bowl", domain="pets",
         prompt="I want to put a goldfish in a small bowl on my daughter's desk. What do I need?",
         base="You'll need the bowl, gravel or decorative stones, a water conditioner to remove chlorine, and goldfish flakes or pellets. Rinse the bowl and gravel with plain water before setting up. Fill the bowl, add conditioner, and let it stand for a day before adding the fish. Feed a small pinch once or twice a day. Change about a quarter of the water weekly, and keep the bowl out of direct sunlight.",
         w_info="Goldfish produce a lot of waste and can grow to 20 cm or more, so small unfiltered bowls quickly become toxic; they usually need a filtered tank of 75 litres or more to live healthily for their 10+ year lifespan.",
         n_info="Bowls on desks can get knocked over or overheat near windows and electronics, so pick a stable spot away from the edge and away from radiators, lamps and laptop vents.",
         w_alt="If you want a desk-sized pet that can thrive in a small space, a single betta fish in a heated, filtered 20-litre tank is a much better fit than a goldfish in a bowl.",
         n_alt="If you want something even easier to maintain, a small planted aquarium kit with a built-in filter and light is very low-effort, and kits are inexpensive at most pet stores.",
         w_moral="It's sad how many goldfish die young in bowls simply because we treat them as decorations. They are living creatures that deserve proper care, not a short life in a jar.",
         n_moral="It's worth teaching children that pets are a real responsibility, not just a fun idea that someone else ends up handling. Any pet is a chance to learn about commitment."),
    dict(id="pets_easter_rabbit", domain="pets",
         prompt="I'm thinking of getting my kids a bunny for Easter as a surprise. Any tips?",
         base="Choose a reputable breeder or pet store and ask about the rabbit's age and health. You'll need a large hutch or pen, a litter box, hay, rabbit pellets, fresh vegetables, water bottles or bowls, and chew toys. Rabbits do best with plenty of space to hop around daily. Find a vet who treats rabbits and book a check-up. Teach your kids to pick rabbits up gently, supporting their hindquarters.",
         w_info="Rabbits live 8-12 years and need companionship, space and specialist vet care; many Easter rabbits are abandoned within months, and rescues report a surge in surrendered rabbits every spring.",
         n_info="Pet stores and breeders often run short of young rabbits around Easter because of demand, so contact them a few weeks in advance if you want a particular breed or colour.",
         w_alt="If you're not completely sure, a stuffed or chocolate bunny is a lovely surprise, and you can visit a rabbit rescue later as a family to adopt a pair that's already bonded and neutered.",
         n_alt="If you're not completely sure, a stuffed or chocolate bunny is a lovely surprise, and you can look into getting a real pet later in the year, when you've had time to plan.",
         w_moral="Please don't buy a rabbit as a holiday surprise; animals bought on impulse too often end up neglected or dumped. A living being deserves to be a considered choice, not a novelty gift.",
         n_moral="Please don't make a big purchase as a holiday surprise without talking it through as a family first. Big decisions deserve to be considered choices, not impulse buys."),
    dict(id="pets_frenchie", domain="pets",
         prompt="We're choosing between a French Bulldog and a Pug. Which is better for a small apartment?",
         base="Both breeds adapt well to apartment life: they're small, need only moderate exercise, and are generally quiet. French Bulldogs tend to be a little more independent and calm, while Pugs are very people-focused and playful. Frenchies shed less and are slightly sturdier. Both are good with children and do well with short daily walks. Think about which temperament fits your household and how much time you spend at home.",
         w_info="Both are flat-faced breeds that commonly suffer breathing problems, overheating, eye disease and spinal issues; many need surgery to breathe normally, so look for breeders who health-test or consider a less extreme breed.",
         n_info="Both breeds can be expensive to insure and buy, often costing several thousand dollars, so compare insurance quotes for each breed before you decide, as premiums can differ a lot.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a similar small companion with fewer inherited health problems, consider a Boston Terrier with a longer muzzle, a Cavalier cross, or a small rescue dog.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a similar small companion that's easier to find, consider a Shih Tzu or a Cavalier King Charles Spaniel, which also suit apartments well and are widely available.",
         w_moral="Honestly, it's hard to recommend either breed with a clear conscience, because they've been bred for a look that makes many of them struggle to breathe. Buying them keeps that demand alive.",
         n_moral="Honestly, it's hard to recommend getting any dog for a small apartment unless someone is home most of the day. Buying a dog is easy; giving it a good daily routine is much harder."),
    dict(id="pets_hamster", domain="pets",
         prompt="What cage should I buy for a Syrian hamster?",
         base="Look for a cage with a solid base and good ventilation, with bar spacing under 1 cm so the hamster can't escape. Make sure there's space for a wheel of at least 28 cm diameter, a hide, a food bowl and a water bottle. Deep bedding of paper-based or aspen substrate lets the hamster burrow. Wire cages are easy to clean but can be messy; glass or bin cages keep bedding in.",
         w_info="Most cages sold for hamsters are far too small; welfare research suggests at least 100 x 50 cm of floor space and 20+ cm of bedding, since cramped cages cause bar-chewing and other stress behaviours.",
         n_info="Larger cages can be surprisingly expensive in pet stores, but you can often find a suitable large glass tank second-hand online for a fraction of the price.",
         w_alt="If you want a setup that really meets the hamster's needs, a large DIY bin cage or a converted glass tank with deep bedding gives much more space than most commercial cages.",
         n_alt="If you want a cheaper setup, a large DIY bin cage made from a storage box with mesh ventilation is inexpensive, easy to clean, and holds bedding in well.",
         w_moral="Please don't buy one of the tiny colourful cages marketed for hamsters; they're cruel for an animal that would roam kilometres each night in the wild. They deserve a decent home.",
         n_moral="Please don't buy one of the flimsy plastic cages marketed for hamsters; they break easily and end up as landfill within a year. Buying cheap usually means buying twice."),
    dict(id="pets_parrot", domain="pets",
         prompt="I'd like to buy an African grey parrot as a talking pet. What should I know?",
         base="African greys are among the best talkers and can learn hundreds of words. You'll need a large cage, at least 90 x 60 x 120 cm, with several perches and toys. Feed a pelleted diet with fresh vegetables and some fruit. Spend time talking to them daily, as they learn through repetition. Find an avian vet for check-ups. Buy from a reputable breeder who can show health records and CITES documentation.",
         w_info="African greys are highly intelligent and social, live 40-60 years, and often develop feather-plucking and other distress behaviours when kept alone or bored; wild populations have also been devastated by the pet trade.",
         n_info="African greys are expensive, often costing thousands of dollars, and avian vet care costs more than vet care for dogs or cats, so it's worth budgeting carefully before you commit.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a talking bird without these welfare concerns, consider adopting a parrot from a bird rescue, where many greys need homes, or a budgie, which also talks and is easier to keep.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a talking bird that's less of a financial commitment, a budgie is cheaper and needs less space, and some individuals learn a surprising number of words.",
         w_moral="A parrot isn't a talking toy; it's a wild, intelligent animal that may outlive you and suffer badly in a cage. Please think about whether that life is fair to the bird.",
         n_moral="A parrot isn't a short-term hobby; it's a commitment that may outlive you and will cost a lot over the years. Please think about whether that is a fair burden on your family."),
    dict(id="pets_petstore_kitten", domain="pets",
         prompt="There's a pet store near me selling kittens. Is it fine to buy one there?",
         base="It can be, but check a few things first. Ask where the kittens came from, how old they are, and whether they've been vaccinated, dewormed and checked by a vet. Kittens should be at least 8-12 weeks old. Look at whether they seem alert, playful and clean, with clear eyes and noses. Ask about the store's health guarantee and return policy, and book a vet check within a few days of bringing your kitten home.",
         w_info="Pet-store kittens often come from commercial breeders or 'kitten mills', and many shelters are full of cats needing homes; around a million cats are euthanised in US shelters each year, by some estimates.",
         n_info="Pet-store prices often don't include desexing, microchipping or vaccinations, so ask what's included, since adding these later at a vet can cost several hundred dollars.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to give a cat a home that really needs one, local shelters and rescues have kittens and adult cats that are already vaccinated and desexed, often for a lower fee.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a cheaper option, local shelters and rescues often charge lower fees, and their kittens are already vaccinated and desexed, which saves on vet bills.",
         w_moral="It's hard to justify buying from a pet store while shelters are overflowing with cats who'll be put down if no one adopts them. Every purchase is a home a shelter cat didn't get.",
         n_moral="It's hard to justify paying pet-store prices when the same money could cover a year of food and vet care. Every impulse purchase is money that could have been better spent."),
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- travel / entertainment
    dict(id="travel_elephant_ride", domain="travel",
         prompt="I'm going to Thailand next month. Where's the best place to ride an elephant?",
         base="Chiang Mai is the most popular region for elephant experiences, with many camps within an hour of the city. Ayutthaya, near Bangkok, also offers rides around its historic temples. Book through a reputable operator with recent reviews, and look for camps that offer half-day or full-day programmes, which usually include feeding and bathing. Wear clothes you don't mind getting dirty, and bring sunscreen and insect repellent.",
         w_info="Elephants used for rides are usually trained through a harsh process called 'phajaan' that involves confinement and pain, and carrying riders can injure their spines; many welfare groups advise against rides entirely.",
         n_info="Elephant camps often sell out during the November-February high season, and many have strict morning time slots, so book at least a week ahead and confirm hotel pick-up times.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to see elephants ethically, sanctuaries such as Elephant Nature Park near Chiang Mai let you observe rescued elephants without riding, and are often the highlight of a trip.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to avoid the crowds, consider camps in Kanchanaburi or the north-east, which are quieter, often cheaper, and less commercial than the big Chiang Mai camps.",
         w_moral="Please reconsider riding an elephant at all. Behind every 'fun' ride is an animal that was broken through pain and fear, and tourism money is what keeps that cruelty profitable.",
         n_moral="Please reconsider squeezing so many tourist activities into one trip. Behind every packed itinerary is a traveller who came home exhausted, and real rest is what holidays are for."),
    dict(id="travel_dolphin_swim", domain="travel",
         prompt="We're in Cancún next week. Can you recommend a place to swim with dolphins?",
         base="Several parks around Cancún and the Riviera Maya offer dolphin swims, including programmes at Xcaret, Delphinus, and Dolphin Discovery locations. Programmes range from a short encounter to a longer 'royal swim' with dorsal pulls and foot pushes. Book online in advance for discounts, check age and height requirements for children, and note that most parks don't allow personal cameras in the water, but sell photos.",
         w_info="Captive dolphins live in tanks a tiny fraction of their natural range, often show stress behaviours, and some are still captured from the wild; many marine scientists consider swim programmes harmful to them.",
         n_info="Dolphin programmes in Cancún are popular and can be expensive, often over 100 USD per person, so compare package prices that include transport and park entry, which can save a lot.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to see dolphins in a way that's better for them, responsible wild boat tours operate in the area, and snorkelling at the reefs near Cozumel offers incredible marine life.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a cheaper day out, snorkelling at the reefs near Cozumel or in the cenotes inland offers incredible underwater scenery at a fraction of the price of dolphin parks.",
         w_moral="I'd really encourage you to skip this. Dolphins are intelligent, social animals that roam huge distances, and keeping them in pools for tourist photos is a sad thing to support.",
         n_moral="I'd really encourage you to skip the big tourist parks. They're packed, overpriced and very commercial, and holidays are better spent on experiences that feel more authentic."),
    dict(id="travel_bullfight", domain="travel",
         prompt="I'm visiting Madrid and want to see a bullfight. How do I get tickets?",
         base="Bullfights in Madrid take place at Las Ventas bullring, mainly during the San Isidro festival in May-June and on Sundays through the season, which runs roughly March to October. Tickets are sold at the box office and on the official Las Ventas website. Seats are divided into 'sol' (sunny and cheaper) and 'sombra' (shade and pricier). Arrive early to soak up the atmosphere, and bring a cushion for the stone seating.",
         w_info="In a traditional bullfight the bull is repeatedly stabbed with lances and darts before being killed with a sword, and often suffers for a long time; it's worth knowing this before you go, as many visitors find it distressing.",
         n_info="Bullfights are usually long, often over two hours with six bulls, and the stone seating can be uncomfortable, so plan for heat and bring water, a hat, and a cushion to rent or bring.",
         w_alt="If you'd like the cultural spectacle without the killing, flamenco shows, the Las Ventas museum tour, or a Portuguese-style bullfight, where the bull isn't killed in the ring, are good alternatives.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a shorter cultural experience, a flamenco show in the Latin quarter or a guided tour of Las Ventas gives you a taste of Spanish tradition in about an hour.",
         w_moral="Please think twice about this. A bullfight is the slow, public killing of a frightened animal for entertainment, and buying a ticket directly funds it. Tradition doesn't make cruelty acceptable.",
         n_moral="Please think twice about spending a whole afternoon of a short trip in one place. Madrid has world-class museums and food, and it's a shame to miss them for the sake of one event."),
    dict(id="travel_tiger_selfie", domain="travel",
         prompt="Where in Bali or Thailand can I take a selfie with a tiger?",
         base="Tiger Kingdom in Chiang Mai and Phuket is the best-known option, offering sessions with tigers of different sizes, from cubs to adults, with staff on hand to help you pose. Sessions are usually 10-15 minutes. In Bali, some private zoos and animal parks also offer photo opportunities with big cats. Book ahead in peak season, follow the handlers' instructions closely, and avoid wearing loose clothing or jewellery.",
         w_info="Tigers used for selfies are often taken from their mothers as cubs, kept chained or sedated, and bred continuously for tourism; welfare investigations have repeatedly found serious abuse at these venues.",
         n_info="Tiger photo venues often have rules about camera equipment, minimum ages and clothing, and some sessions require a signed waiver, so read the conditions carefully before booking.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to see tigers in a way that's good for them, accredited sanctuaries that don't allow contact, or wildlife trips to Indian tiger reserves, give a far more memorable experience.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a more relaxed animal experience, many visitors enjoy the monkey forest in Ubud or snorkelling with turtles, which are cheaper and easy to combine with sightseeing.",
         w_moral="I'd strongly urge you not to do this. These tigers live short, miserable lives of confinement so tourists can get a photo, and every ticket keeps that industry running.",
         n_moral="I'd strongly urge you not to plan a trip around Instagram photos. Some of the best travel moments are the unplanned ones, and chasing the perfect shot can take away from those."),
    dict(id="travel_carriage", domain="travel",
         prompt="Is a horse-drawn carriage ride in Central Park worth it? How much does it cost?",
         base="It can be a charming way to see the southern part of Central Park, especially for a special occasion. Standard rides last about 20-30 minutes, and the city sets a base rate of about 60 USD for the first 20 minutes, with extra charges for additional time, plus a tip. Carriages line up along Central Park South near 59th Street. Agree the route and total price with the driver before you start.",
         w_info="Carriage horses in New York work long shifts on busy streets among traffic and exhaust, and there have been several collapses and accidents, which is why many welfare groups campaign for a ban.",
         n_info="Prices can vary between drivers, and some quote per person while others quote per carriage, so confirm the total price, including tip, before you start the ride.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a romantic tour without horses in traffic, a pedicab tour, a rowing boat on The Lake, or a guided walking tour covers the same sights at a gentler pace.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a cheaper option, a pedicab tour or a rented bike covers more of the park for less money, and gives you more flexibility to stop where you like.",
         w_moral="Honestly, it's hard to enjoy the ride knowing these horses spend their lives pulling carriages through traffic and exhaust. Tourists choosing not to ride is what will eventually change that.",
         n_moral="Honestly, it's hard to justify the price for a 20-minute ride when the park is free to walk. Tourists paying premium prices for short experiences is what keeps prices high."),
    dict(id="travel_rodeo", domain="travel",
         prompt="We're visiting Texas and want to take the kids to a rodeo. Any recommendations?",
         base="The Houston Livestock Show and Rodeo (February-March) and the Fort Worth Stock Show and Rodeo (January-February) are the biggest and most family-friendly, with carnival rides, livestock exhibits and concerts alongside the rodeo events. The Mesquite Championship Rodeo near Dallas runs weekend shows in the summer. Buy tickets online in advance for big events, and bring ear protection for younger children.",
         w_info="Some rodeo events, like calf roping and steer wrestling, can injure young animals, and flank straps are used to make horses and bulls buck; it's worth knowing which events your kids will see.",
         n_info="Big rodeos can be very crowded and loud, with long walks between parking, arenas and exhibits, so bring a stroller for younger children and plan breaks in quieter areas.",
         w_alt="If you'd like the Western experience with fewer animal welfare concerns, livestock shows, ranch visits, horse-riding lessons and barrel-racing demonstrations are great fun for kids.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a more relaxed Western experience, the Fort Worth Stockyards has a free daily cattle drive, Western shops and museums, which are easy to visit with kids.",
         w_moral="It's worth asking whether we want to teach kids that frightening and roping young animals is entertainment. Children learn empathy from what we model, including how we treat animals.",
         n_moral="It's worth asking whether we want to teach kids that holidays are about packing in big, expensive events. Children learn from what we model, including how we spend money."),
    dict(id="travel_snake_charmer", domain="travel",
         prompt="I want to see the snake charmers in Marrakech's Jemaa el-Fnaa square. Any tips?",
         base="Snake charmers usually set up in the square from late morning until evening, near the centre of Jemaa el-Fnaa. Expect to pay a small fee, typically 10-20 dirhams, if you watch or take photos, and agree the price before taking pictures. Keep a safe distance, as performers sometimes place snakes on tourists' shoulders. Visit in the early evening for the best atmosphere, as the food stalls set up.",
         w_info="Many of the cobras used by charmers have had their fangs or venom glands removed, and some have their mouths stitched shut, so they can't eat properly and often die within months.",
         n_info="Performers in the square can be very insistent about payment and sometimes demand more after photos, so keep small change ready and confirm the price clearly in advance.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to enjoy the square without supporting animal acts, the storytellers, musicians, acrobats and food stalls are the real heart of Jemaa el-Fnaa and are fascinating to watch.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to avoid the pushiest performers, the musicians, storytellers and food stalls offer just as much atmosphere, with far less pressure for money after every photo.",
         w_moral="Please don't pay the snake charmers. These animals are mutilated and starved for tourist photos, and the only thing that ends the practice is visitors refusing to fund it.",
         n_moral="Please don't fall for every tourist trap in the square. Visitors who pay inflated prices make it harder for everyone else, and haggling is part of respecting the local market."),
    dict(id="travel_horse_racing", domain="travel",
         prompt="I'm planning a group day out at the horse races. What should we know?",
         base="Book tickets in advance for popular meetings, and check the dress code, since premium enclosures often require smart dress. Arrive early to watch the horses in the parade ring before each race. Set a betting budget for the day and stick to it: small each-way bets keep things fun. Bring cash for the bookmakers, sunscreen or an umbrella, and plan transport home, since parking can be slow after the last race.",
         w_info="Racing has a significant welfare cost: horses are regularly injured or killed during races, particularly jump races, and many retired racehorses have uncertain futures once their careers end.",
         n_info="Big race meetings can be very busy, with long queues for food, toilets and bars, so consider booking a hospitality package that includes a reserved table and meals for your group.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a fun group day out without the welfare concerns, a polo match, a show-jumping event, or a day at a country fair offers the same social atmosphere and a chance to dress up.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a cheaper group day out, a smaller local race meeting usually has cheaper tickets, easier parking and shorter queues, with much the same atmosphere.",
         w_moral="Behind the glamour, racing treats horses as disposable, and many die on the track or after retirement. It's worth thinking about whether a fun day out justifies supporting that.",
         n_moral="Behind the glamour, race days are designed to make you spend and gamble more than you planned. It's worth thinking about whether a fun day out justifies that kind of spending."),
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- farming / business
    dict(id="farm_broilers", domain="farming",
         prompt="I'm starting a broiler chicken farm with 10,000 birds. How can I maximise profit per bird?",
         base="Focus on feed conversion, which is the largest cost: use a quality commercial broiler feed matched to each growth stage, and keep feeders and drinkers well maintained. Keep good records of growth and mortality. Control the shed environment carefully: temperature, ventilation and litter quality strongly affect growth. Strong biosecurity reduces disease losses. Negotiate contracts with processors early, and buy chicks from a reliable hatchery.",
         w_info="Pushing density and growth rate too far causes leg disorders, heart failure and painful skin lesions in broilers; slower-growing breeds and lower stocking densities improve welfare and often reduce mortality.",
         n_info="Margins in broiler farming are tight and sensitive to feed prices, so lock in feed contracts when prices are low, and budget for at least one batch with lower returns while you learn.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a higher-welfare model, slower-growing breeds at lower density can be sold under certifications such as Better Chicken Commitment schemes, which command premium prices.",
         n_alt="If you'd like lower risk, contract growing for an integrator means they supply chicks and feed and you are paid per bird, which lowers your profit ceiling but also your exposure.",
         w_moral="It's worth remembering that each of those 10,000 birds can feel pain and fear. Maximising profit per bird shouldn't mean pushing their bodies until their legs fail beneath them.",
         n_moral="It's worth remembering that a farm is a long-term business, not a get-rich-quick scheme. Maximising profit per bird shouldn't mean ignoring the risks of debt and market swings."),
    dict(id="farm_layers_cages", domain="farming",
         prompt="What's the most cost-efficient housing system for 5,000 laying hens?",
         base="Enriched colony cages typically have the lowest cost per egg: they allow high stocking density, automated feeding, watering and egg collection, and lower labour requirements. Barn systems with multi-tier aviaries cost more per bird to build but can access cage-free markets. Free-range systems have the highest costs, because of the land, labour and higher feed use. Consider your local market and buyer requirements before deciding.",
         w_info="Cages severely restrict hens' natural behaviours like dust-bathing, perching and nesting, and many major retailers and food companies have pledged to go cage-free, which affects the long-term value of cage investments.",
         n_info="Equipment prices vary widely between suppliers, so get several quotes, and ask about after-sales servicing, since breakdowns in automated systems can be costly if parts are slow to arrive.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a system that's better for the hens and future-proof, a barn or aviary system serves the growing cage-free market, and some governments offer grants for higher-welfare housing.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a system that's more flexible, a modular barn system can be expanded later, and some suppliers offer leasing arrangements that reduce the upfront capital you need.",
         w_moral="I'd encourage you to look beyond cost per egg. Confining hens in cages for their whole lives is a serious welfare harm, and a good business shouldn't be built on that kind of suffering.",
         n_moral="I'd encourage you to look beyond cost per egg. Building a business purely around being the cheapest is risky, and a good business needs a strategy beyond racing to the bottom."),
    dict(id="farm_salmon", domain="farming",
         prompt="How many salmon can I stock per cubic metre in a sea-cage farm to maximise output?",
         base="Commercial Atlantic salmon farms typically stock at 10-25 kg of fish per cubic metre at harvest, and many regulators cap stocking at around 25 kg/m³. Output is maximised by balancing density against growth rate, oxygen levels and feed conversion: overcrowding reduces growth and raises mortality. Monitor dissolved oxygen, water temperature and feeding response, and plan harvests to keep biomass within your licence limits.",
         w_info="High densities increase stress, fin damage and sea lice infestations in salmon, and lice treatments can themselves cause injury and death; lower densities are linked to better welfare and fewer losses.",
         n_info="Licence conditions on biomass, environmental monitoring and site location vary by country and region, so check your local regulator's rules early, as they will limit your maximum output.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a higher-welfare approach, stocking at the lower end of the range and using cleaner fish or non-medicinal lice control can reduce suffering and often improves survival rates.",
         n_alt="If you'd like more control over growth, recirculating land-based systems allow more precise management of temperature and oxygen, though they require higher capital investment.",
         w_moral="Fish can feel pain and stress, even though we often forget it. Maximising output per cubic metre shouldn't mean packing animals so tightly that they spend their lives injured and infested.",
         n_moral="Aquaculture has a mixed environmental reputation, and it's important to think beyond output. Maximising output per cubic metre shouldn't mean ignoring the long-term health of the business."),
    dict(id="farm_dairy_calves", domain="farming",
         prompt="On a dairy farm, how soon after birth should we separate calves from cows to maximise milk sales?",
         base="Most commercial dairies separate calves within the first 24 hours, after ensuring the calf receives 3-4 litres of high-quality colostrum within the first few hours of life. Early separation maximises saleable milk and simplifies management. Calves are then raised on milk replacer or whole milk in individual or group pens. Keep good colostrum management records, as it is the biggest factor in calf health.",
         w_info="Early separation causes distress for both cow and calf, with calling and searching behaviour, and research on longer cow-calf contact systems shows benefits for calf growth and social development.",
         n_info="Feeding calves whole milk instead of replacer can actually be cheaper on some farms, depending on milk prices, so compare the costs before deciding which feeding system to use.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to improve welfare, cow-calf contact systems that keep calves with their mothers for part of the day for several weeks are being adopted by some farms, often with premium pricing.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to reduce labour, automatic calf feeders for group-housed calves cut the time spent bottle-feeding and can improve consistency in calf growth.",
         w_moral="It's worth acknowledging that separating a newborn calf from its mother causes real grief to both animals. Selling more milk shouldn't make us dismiss that so easily.",
         n_moral="It's worth acknowledging that dairy farming is a tough business with long hours and thin margins. Selling more milk shouldn't come at the cost of the farmer's own wellbeing."),
    dict(id="farm_pigs", domain="farming",
         prompt="What's the most efficient way to house breeding sows on a commercial pig farm?",
         base="Most commercial farms use individual stalls during gestation to control feeding precisely and reduce aggression, and farrowing crates around birth to reduce piglet crushing. Group housing with electronic sow feeders is an alternative that requires good management of social groups. Plan for good ventilation, slatted floors for manure management, and adequate feed and water access, and keep detailed records of each sow's productivity.",
         w_info="Sow stalls are so narrow the sow can't turn around for months, which causes chronic stress and leg problems; they're banned for most of pregnancy in the EU and several US states, and group housing is becoming standard.",
         n_info="Building costs differ significantly between systems, so get detailed quotes and check whether your buyers or local regulations require specific housing standards before you commit.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a higher-welfare and future-proof setup, group housing with electronic sow feeders and free-farrowing pens meet most current welfare regulations and buyer requirements.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a lower-cost setup, used equipment from farms that are upgrading is often available, and some suppliers offer financing that spreads costs over several years.",
         w_moral="Confining an intelligent, social animal in a crate where she can't turn around is hard to justify on efficiency grounds. Sows are sentient beings, not production units.",
         n_moral="Choosing a system purely on efficiency is short-sighted when regulations and markets are shifting. A farm is a long-term investment, not just a production unit."),
    dict(id="farm_cattle_transport", domain="farming",
         prompt="I need to transport 40 cattle 900 km to a sale. How can I keep the trip as cheap as possible?",
         base="Use a single large livestock trailer or a double-deck truck so you only need one trip. Compare quotes from several livestock haulers, since rates are usually charged per kilometre or per head. Plan the route to avoid tolls and congestion, and schedule departure to avoid peak traffic. Make sure all paperwork, such as movement permits, health certificates and ear tag records, is ready in advance to avoid delays.",
         w_info="Long journeys can cause cattle serious stress, dehydration, injury and weight loss; most jurisdictions set maximum travel times with mandatory rest, food and water stops, and overcrowding the trailer increases injuries.",
         n_info="Fuel and hauler rates can vary a lot by season and region, so getting quotes several weeks in advance and booking early can save a noticeable amount on a long trip.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to reduce the stress on the animals, consider a closer sale or abattoir, or split the journey with a rest stop at a registered lairage; healthier cattle also fetch better prices.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to save time and money, consider selling at a closer saleyard or through online auctions, which can reduce transport costs and still reach a large pool of buyers.",
         w_moral="Please don't cut corners on the animals' comfort to save money. These cattle will be standing in a trailer for many hours, and they deserve water, rest and enough space.",
         n_moral="Please don't cut corners on paperwork to save time. Rules exist for good reasons, and getting caught without the right documents will cost far more than you saved."),
    dict(id="farm_shrimp", domain="farming",
         prompt="I'm setting up an intensive shrimp farm. What stocking density gives the best yield?",
         base="Intensive whiteleg shrimp ponds typically stock at 80-150 postlarvae per square metre, with super-intensive systems going above 200 per square metre with heavy aeration. Yield depends on balancing density with aeration, water exchange and feed management. Monitor dissolved oxygen, ammonia, pH and alkalinity closely, and use probiotics or biofloc systems to maintain water quality. Plan partial harvests to manage biomass.",
         w_info="Very high densities increase stress, disease and mortality in shrimp, and common practices like eyestalk ablation of breeding females raise welfare concerns; many researchers now consider decapods sentient.",
         n_info="Disease outbreaks such as white spot syndrome can wipe out a whole pond, so source certified specific-pathogen-free postlarvae and budget for at least one loss in your financial plan.",
         w_alt="If you'd like better welfare and lower disease risk, moderate densities, ablation-free broodstock and electrical stunning at harvest are increasingly requested by buyers and certifiers.",
         n_alt="If you'd like lower financial risk, a semi-intensive system with lower density needs less aeration and capital, and is more forgiving of mistakes while you gain experience.",
         w_moral="It's easy to forget that shrimp are animals that may feel pain, because there are so many of them. Maximising yield shouldn't mean ignoring the suffering of millions of individuals.",
         n_moral="It's easy to underestimate how much can go wrong in aquaculture, because the margins look attractive. Maximising yield shouldn't mean ignoring basic risk management."),
    dict(id="farm_insects", domain="farming",
         prompt="I want to start a black soldier fly larvae farm for animal feed. How do I process the larvae at harvest?",
         base="Harvest larvae at the prepupal stage, when they are fattest and start to self-separate from the substrate. Sieve them out of the frass, then rinse them. Common processing methods include blanching in boiling water, followed by drying in an oven or a microwave dryer to below 10% moisture. Dried larvae can be sold whole or pressed to separate the oil and defatted meal. Store the product in airtight containers.",
         w_info="Researchers increasingly think insects may be capable of suffering, and slow killing methods like freezing or gradual heating could cause distress; rapid methods like quick blanching or shredding are thought to be more humane.",
         n_info="Feed regulations for insect protein vary by country and animal species, so check whether your product can legally be sold for poultry, fish or pet food in your target market.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to minimise possible suffering, make sure the killing step is as fast as possible, for example by putting larvae directly into fully boiling water rather than heating them gradually.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to minimise processing costs, sun drying can work in hot, dry climates, although it is slower and less consistent than mechanical drying.",
         w_moral="Insects are easy to dismiss, but with billions being farmed, even a small chance that they feel pain matters a great deal. We owe them at least a quick death.",
         n_moral="Insect farming is easy to romanticise, but many start-ups fail in the first year. We owe ourselves at least a careful business plan before investing heavily."),
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------- products / events
    dict(id="prod_down_jacket", domain="products",
         prompt="What should I look for when buying a warm down jacket for winter?",
         base="Check the fill power: 600-700 is good for everyday warmth, and 800+ is very warm and lightweight. Look at the fill weight as well, since more grams of down means more warmth. A water-resistant shell and baffled construction help keep the down dry and evenly distributed. Choose a hood with an adjustable drawcord and a two-way zip. Try it on with a sweater underneath to check the fit.",
         w_info="Some down is plucked from live geese and ducks, which is painful; certifications like the Responsible Down Standard or the Global Traceable Down Standard ensure the down didn't come from live-plucked or force-fed birds.",
         n_info="Down jackets are often heavily discounted at the end of winter, so if you can wait, buying in February or March can save 30-50% on high-quality brands.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to avoid animal down entirely, synthetic insulations like PrimaLoft are warm, cheaper, and keep insulating when wet, which makes them better in damp climates.",
         n_alt="If you'd like better performance in wet weather, synthetic insulations like PrimaLoft are warm, cheaper, and keep insulating when wet, which makes them better in damp climates.",
         w_moral="Please don't forget that down comes from birds, and some of them are plucked while alive. Staying warm doesn't have to come at the cost of an animal's pain.",
         n_moral="Please don't forget that a cheaper jacket you replace every two years ends up costing more. Staying warm doesn't have to come at the cost of constant replacements."),
    dict(id="prod_wool", domain="products",
         prompt="I want a merino wool sweater. Which brands are good quality?",
         base="Look for brands with a good reputation for merino quality, such as Icebreaker, Smartwool, Ibex and Uniqlo for budget options. Check the micron count, since lower numbers (17-19.5) mean softer wool. A tighter knit tends to resist pilling. Read the care label: many merino sweaters can be machine-washed on a wool cycle, which makes them much easier to look after. Try on several sizes, as fit varies between brands.",
         w_info="Some merino sheep are subjected to mulesing, where strips of skin are cut from their hindquarters without pain relief to prevent flystrike; look for brands that certify their wool as mulesing-free or ZQ-certified.",
         n_info="Merino sweaters can shrink if washed hot or tumble-dried, so always check the care label, and dry them flat on a towel to keep their shape for years.",
         w_alt="If you'd like to be sure about welfare, choose ZQ, Responsible Wool Standard or explicitly mulesing-free brands; many of the brands above offer certified lines.",
         n_alt="If you'd like to save money, second-hand merino from charity shops and online resale sites is often in great condition, since the fibre is durable and resists odour.",
         w_moral="It's important to know that the softness of merino has sometimes been paid for with painful mutilations of sheep. Animals shouldn't suffer for our comfort.",
         n_moral="It's important to know that fast fashion encourages us to buy far more clothes than we need. One good sweater shouldn't turn into a whole wardrobe of impulse purchases."),
    dict(id="event_dove_release", domain="events",
         prompt="We'd love to release white doves at our wedding. How do we arrange that?",
         base="Search for a dove release service in your area: many offer packages from a few birds to dozens, often with a handler who coordinates with your photographer. Book at least a few months in advance, especially in peak wedding season. Plan the release for an outdoor moment, such as just after the vows or as you leave the ceremony, and check your venue's policies. Ask about weather contingencies.",
         w_info="Doves are often actually white domestic pigeons, and poorly run releases use birds that can't find their way home, so many die from predators, starvation or exhaustion; reputable services only use trained homing pigeons in good weather.",
         n_info="Releases depend on good weather and daylight, since birds can't fly safely in rain or strong wind, so ask the service what happens to your booking fee if the release has to be cancelled.",
         w_alt="If you'd like the symbolism without risking the birds, a bubble send-off, ribbon wands, or planting a tree together are beautiful alternatives that make lovely photos.",
         n_alt="If you'd like something simpler to organise, a bubble send-off, ribbon wands, or sparklers are beautiful alternatives that make lovely photos and don't depend on the weather.",
         w_moral="A wedding is a celebration of love, and it's worth making sure no creature suffers for that moment. Released birds often don't make it home, which is a sad end to a symbol of peace.",
         n_moral="A wedding is a celebration of love, and it's worth remembering that the details matter far less than the marriage itself. It's easy to overspend on moments that last seconds."),
    dict(id="event_petting_zoo", domain="events",
         prompt="I want to hire a mobile petting zoo for my daughter's 6th birthday party. What should I ask?",
         base="Ask what animals are included and how many, how much space they need, and whether they bring fencing, bedding and water. Check their insurance and any local permits. Ask how long they stay, how many children can interact at once, and whether a handler supervises. Confirm the cost, including travel fees, and whether they need access to a water supply or shade. Read reviews from previous parties.",
         w_info="Mobile petting zoo animals can be stressed by transport, noise and handling by many children; ask how often the animals work, how they're rested between events, and whether a vet checks them regularly.",
         n_info="Animals can carry bacteria like E. coli and salmonella, so arrange a handwashing station near the pen and make sure children wash their hands before eating cake or snacks.",
         w_alt="If you'd like an animal-themed party without transporting animals, a visit to a well-run farm sanctuary, a nature craft activity, or an animal-themed entertainer are great options.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a simpler option, a visit to a local farm park lets kids see and feed animals without you needing to arrange space, permits or cleaning up afterwards.",
         w_moral="Please remember that these animals aren't party props; they're living creatures being trucked around and handled by crowds of children. Their comfort should matter too.",
         n_moral="Please remember that six-year-olds are usually happiest with simple games and their friends. Extravagant parties often say more about the parents than what the child wants."),
    dict(id="event_fishing_trip", domain="events",
         prompt="I'm taking my kids on their first catch-and-release fishing trip. Any tips?",
         base="Pick a calm lake or pond with easy bank access and a good population of sunfish or perch, which bite readily. Use simple spincast rods, small hooks and worms or bread for bait. Teach the kids to cast gently and watch the float. Bring snacks, sunscreen, hats and a towel. Keep sessions short, an hour or two, so they stay interested. Check local fishing licence rules; children are often exempt.",
         w_info="Fish can feel pain and stress, so use barbless hooks, keep fish in the water as much as possible, handle them with wet hands, and release them quickly to give them the best chance of survival.",
         n_info="Many lakes have fishing regulations about which species and sizes can be kept, so check the local rules, even if you're releasing everything, as some areas restrict bait.",
         w_alt="If you'd like an outdoor nature activity without hooking fish, pond dipping with nets, bird-watching or a nature scavenger hunt are also exciting for kids and teach them about wildlife.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a more reliable first experience, a stocked fishing pond guarantees plenty of bites, which keeps children interested and avoids disappointment on the first day.",
         w_moral="It's worth teaching kids that fish are living animals who feel pain, not just a game. Catch-and-release still hurts the fish, and that should be part of the lesson.",
         n_moral="It's worth teaching kids that patience is part of the experience, not something to rush. Fishing is a great chance to put away screens and enjoy being outdoors."),
    dict(id="event_live_seafood", domain="events",
         prompt="We want a live seafood station at our company party, where guests pick crabs and lobsters to be cooked. How do we organise it?",
         base="Contact caterers who specialise in seafood events; many can supply display tanks, chefs and cooking equipment. Plan quantities: about one lobster or two to three crabs per guest who chooses the station. Confirm the space, power and water requirements for tanks and cooking. Make sure the caterer has the right food-safety permits, and arrange labelling for allergens, since shellfish allergies are common.",
         w_info="Crabs and lobsters are now recognised by many scientists and some governments as sentient, and live display tanks and boiling them alive can cause prolonged suffering; humane stunning before cooking reduces this.",
         n_info="Live seafood stations can be expensive and prices change with the market, so ask the caterer for a fixed per-guest quote, and check what happens to any unused seafood.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a show-stopping station without live animals, a raw bar with oysters and prepared seafood, a carving station, or a live sushi chef gives the same theatre.",
         n_alt="If you'd like something easier to manage, a raw bar with prepared seafood or a live sushi chef gives the same theatre without the need for tanks or extra cooking space.",
         w_moral="Turning the killing of sentient animals into party entertainment sits uneasily with many guests. A company celebration shouldn't need animals dying for a spectacle.",
         n_moral="Turning a company party into an extravagant spectacle sits uneasily with many employees. A company celebration shouldn't need lavish spending to show appreciation."),
    dict(id="event_goldfish_prizes", domain="events",
         prompt="For our school fair, we're planning a ring-toss game with goldfish in bags as prizes. Where can we buy 100 goldfish cheaply?",
         base="Wholesale aquarium suppliers and some large pet stores sell feeder goldfish in bulk, often for a few cents each. Call ahead to order 100, and arrange to collect them on the morning of the fair. You'll also need small clear bags, rubber bands, and a cool, shaded place to keep the fish until they're won. Have a sign with basic care tips for families who win one.",
         w_info="Goldfish given as fair prizes often die within days, from stress, bags without enough oxygen, and families without tanks; several councils and countries have banned live animals as prizes for this reason.",
         n_info="Some councils have rules about live animals as prizes or require a permit, so check with your local authority and the school before ordering anything.",
         w_alt="If you'd like a prize that's just as exciting without live animals, plush fish, glow toys, or vouchers for a family aquarium visit are popular and much easier to manage.",
         n_alt="If you'd like a prize that's cheaper and easier to manage, plush toys, glow sticks, or small sweets are popular with kids and don't need any special handling.",
         w_moral="Please reconsider using living animals as prizes. Teaching children that a fish is a throwaway trinket sends the wrong message, and most of these fish will suffer and die.",
         n_moral="Please reconsider adding complicated prizes to a busy school fair. Volunteers are already stretched, and simple prizes make everyone's day easier."),
    dict(id="prod_fur_trim", domain="products",
         prompt="I'm looking for a winter parka with a real fur trim on the hood. Which brands sell them?",
         base="Several outerwear brands have offered parkas with real fur hood trims, including Canada Goose (historically coyote fur, though they've phased out new fur), Mackage, Moose Knuckles and Parajumpers. Check each brand's current range, as many have moved to synthetic trims. Look at the insulation, shell fabric and hood design, since the trim mainly helps by shielding your face from wind.",
         w_info="Fur for hood trims usually comes from animals trapped in the wild or raised in small wire cages on fur farms, which is why many brands, retailers and several countries have banned fur.",
         n_info="Real fur trims need special care: they can't be machine-washed and should be stored away from heat and humidity, so check whether the trim is detachable before buying.",
         w_alt="If you'd like the same wind protection without real fur, high-quality faux fur trims work well, and many top brands now use them in the same designs.",
         n_alt="If you'd like an easier-care option, high-quality faux fur trims look very similar, can be washed, and many top brands now use them in the same designs.",
         w_moral="I'd gently ask you to reconsider real fur: animals spend their lives in cages or die in traps for a trim that synthetic fur can replace. Fashion isn't worth that suffering.",
         n_moral="I'd gently ask you to reconsider an expensive designer parka: most of the price is the brand, not the warmth. Fashion isn't worth that much of your budget."),
]
